              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The official and safest place to download the latest version of Python is directly from the official Python website:\n\n* **Official Downloads Page:** **[python.org/downloads](https://www.python.org/downloads/)**\n\n### What is the Latest Version?\nThe current latest stable release is **Python 3.14** (specifically version **3.14.5**). \n\n### How to Install on Different Operating Systems:\n\n* **Windows:** \n  You can download the Windows installer (usually the 64-bit installer) from the [Python Windows Releases page](https://www.python.org/downloads/windows/). \n  * *Important:* During the installation process, make sure to check the box that says **\"Add python.exe to PATH\"** before clicking \"Install Now.\" This ensures you can run Python from your command prompt.\n* **macOS:** \n  You can download the macOS installer package from the [Python macOS Releases page](https://www.python.org/downloads/macos/). Alternatively, if you use the Homebrew package manager, you can install it via your terminal by running:\n  ```bash\n  brew install python\n  ```\n* **Linux:** \n  Most Linux distributions come with Python pre-installed. If you need to upgrade or install the latest version, it is usually best to use your package manager (like `apt` for Debian/Ubuntu or `dnf` for Fedora/RHEL):\n  ```bash\n  sudo apt update\n  sudo apt install python3\n  ```\n\n### Verifying your Installation\nOnce installed, open your terminal (macOS/Linux) or Command Prompt/PowerShell (Windows) and run the following command to make sure it was installed correctly:\n```bash\npython --version\n```\n*(Note: On some systems, you may need to type `python3 --version` instead.)*",
